\ Baseline probe: the linter does not yet fully AOT-compile. Private
\ linter / lexer words reached through `run-lint` are rejected by the C
\ emitter because their bodies use row-variable stack shapes the AOT
\ compiler cannot model yet: stack shuffles and `dip` reaching below a
\ word's declared inputs into the symbolic row, `#nth!` indexed mutation,
\ and `call`s to quotations of unknown effect (rule `check>>` / `match:`
\ quotations and `#each` / `#map` bodies).
\
\ Folding literal-count `array-n` into a concrete-arity array construction
\ unblocked the two pack words whose downstream ops then sat above a precise
\ stack rather than a collapsed row (`(lint-file)` and
\ `(make-reciprocal-cycle-record)`), so the set shrank from eight words to
\ six.
\
\ Modeling concrete-arity ops that reach below a word's declared inputs into
\ the implicit caller row -- emitting them against the live stack and
\ collapsing to a row instead of rejecting as an abstract underflow --
\ unblocked `(file-use-targets-only)` (its `nip` reaches below its one input),
\ so the set shrank from six words to five. Compiling `call`s to
\ quotations of unknown effect together with the surrounding row control flow
\ then unblocked the rule and circular-dependency words, shrinking the set to
\ two: `(lexer-step)` and `(extract-use-targets)`.
\
\ `(extract-use-targets)`'s body compiles, but its declared effect under-counted
\ its outputs -- the body leaves `path targets`, not just `targets`, and the AOT
\ epilogue requires the final stack depth to match the declared output count.
\ Correcting that declaration (and the same under-count on its caller
\ `(file-use-targets)`) left only `(lexer-step)`, whose blocker is a
\ variable-arity callee.
\
\ Recognizing `(try-rules)` as variable-arity (so a caller collapses its result
\ to a row instead of trusting the declared two outputs) and then emitting the
\ inline `swap` that reorders that row as a live native swap pinned at slot 0
\ re-admits both `(build-semantic-context)` and `(lexer-step)`, so the
\ row-variable blocker set is now empty -- the per-word "could not be compiled"
\ inventory is gone.
\
\ With the row-variable wall cleared, the build now halts at a separate blocker
\ that is not about row-variable codegen: the AOT image serializer cannot encode
\ a `struct_instance` value, and the lint rules registry is a mutable map holding
\ rule records as struct instances. This surfaces as a generic
\ "Error generating C source: NotCompilable". Encoding struct instances into the
\ image is its own concern, tracked with the full lint-driver build rather than
\ here; this golden pins that struct-instance serialization is the current
\ residual.
use "lint" ;
use "lint-rules" ;
{ } run-lint drop
